  "source_document": {
    "document_id": "c29e10d9-fa54-4e80-bea8-7d1adad429e9",
    "origin_url": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/23/bmws-ix4-promises-428-miles-range-and-ultra-fast-charging/",
    "collected_at": "2026-11-23T16:37:59.789149+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/23/bmws-ix4-promises-428-miles-range-and-ultra-fast-charging/",
    "canonical_url": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/23/bmws-ix4-promises-428-miles-range-and-ultra-fast-charging/",
    "origin_publisher": "Electrek",
    "published_at": "2026-11-23T15:21:00+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-23T15:21:00+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-23T16:39:26.910665+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-23T16:39:26.910665+00:00",
    "body": "BMW’s new iX4 is almost here, and it could make range anxiety a thing of the past. With 428 miles of EPA range, more than 500 miles on the WLTP cycle, and ultra-fast charging, the new EV could make road trips a whole lot easier.\nBMW iX4 enters production ahead of March 2027 launch\nSeries production of the new iX4 is officially underway at BMW Group’s manufacturing plant in Debrecen, Hungary.\nThe iX4 is the second fully electric BMW model built at the facility, following the record-breaking production ramp-up of the new iX3 SUV.\nBMW said the plant began series production of an entirely new model in less than two months after the company introduced a 24-hour, three-shift schedule to ramp up output to meet higher-than-expected demand.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nThe new iX4 will go on sale in March 2027 as an all-electric replacement for the X4. It will be available in two variants: the BMW iX4 50 xDrive and BMW iX4 40 xDrive, with a range-topping M60 xDrive set to follow shortly after.\nBoth versions use a dual-motor all-wheel-drive (AWD) powertrain. The iX4 50 xDrive provides a combined 468 hp and 475 lb-ft of torque, enabling it to sprint from 0 to 60 mph in 4.7 seconds.\nBMW launches series production of the new iX4 (Source: BMW)\nThe performance M60 version delivers a combined 603 hp and 645 lb-ft of torque, which BMW says is good for a 0 to 60 mph sprint in just 3.9 seconds.\nThe iX4 features a distinct coupe-SUV-like silhouette rather than the traditional boxy, upright SUV design of the iX3. With a rakish roofline and an aerodynamic design, the new iX4 has a drag coefficient of just 0.23.\nLike the iX3, the iX4 is based on BMW’s Neue Klasse architecture with its sixth-generation (Gen6) eDrive system.\nBMW iX4 M60 (left) and 50 xDrive (right) (Source: BMW)\nWhile EPA figures have yet to be finalized, BMW said the new iX4 has an estimated driving range of up to 428 miles on a single charge using a 112.2 kWh battery. Under WLTP conditions, it offers an all-electric range of up to 514 miles (828 km).\nThanks to its 800V architecture, BMW’s new iX4 can add up to 184 miles of range (384 km on the WLTP cycle) in just 10 minutes with charging speeds of up to 400 kW. Charging the vehicle from 10% to 80% takes about 21 minutes.\nBMW iX4 interior (Source: BMW)\nLike BMW’s other Neue Klasse models, the new iX4 features its updated interior design. The setup includes a large 17.9″ infotainment screen and BMW’s new Panoramic Vision, a 42″ display spanning the base of the windshield.\nThe iX4 is 188.3″ long, 74.6″ wide, and 63.3″ tall, with a 114.1″ wheelbase, which is about the same size as the iX3. However, it’s slightly lower than the iX3 thanks to the sloped roofline.\nLike all Gen6 BMW models, the new iX4 supports bidirectional charging, enabling it to be used as a mobile power source for e-bikes, camping equipment, or other electrical devices.\nThe new BMW iX4 50 xDrive will go on sale in March 2027, starting at $66,300, or $4,800 more than the iX3 50 xDrive. That doesn’t include the $1,495 destination fee.\nIn Germany, the new BMW iX4 40 xDrive starts at €70,900 ($77,500), and in the UK it’s priced from £53,250 ($69,500), OTR.\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on X , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
